## Is peripheral arterial disease a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. If PAD limits how far you can walk, you are likely to be covered.

## Reasonable adjustments

- a parking space close to the entrance
- less walking or standing, and the chance to sit down
- time off for appointments, exercise programmes and surgery

## Benefits

PIP is based on how PAD affects you on most days. The main activity affected is **moving around**. Think about:

- how far you can walk before the pain makes you stop, measured in metres
- whether you could walk the same distance again soon after
- how slowly you walk and whether you need a stick or other aid

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you are over State Pension age, look at [Attendance Allowance](/benefits/attendance-allowance/) instead of PIP. It is for people who need help with personal care or supervision.

## If you cannot work

If your health means you cannot work, or can only work a little, you may be able to get [New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/) or extra money in [Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/). These are separate from PIP, and you can get them at the same time.

## Blue Badge

If you have serious difficulty walking, you may be able to get a [Blue Badge](/support/travel/blue-badge/) for parking.

Free, independent advisers, such as **Citizens Advice** or a local welfare rights service, can help you check what you can claim and fill in forms.
